# Debug a path: git check-ignore -v <file>
#
# Output directories are anchored to the repository root with a leading slash. A bare `build/` or
# `site/` matches at every depth, and Odoo.sh has builds: an unanchored rule would silently keep a
# `odouche/build/` subpackage out of the repository.

# --- Python artifacts ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
/.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# --- Coverage ---
.coverage
.coverage.*
/coverage.xml
/htmlcov/

# --- Build and distribution artifacts ---
/build/
/dist/

# --- Documentation site and its build cache (zensical) ---
/site/
/.cache/

# --- Captured traffic ---
# A browser capture of the Odoo.sh web interface is how upstream behaviour gets worked out, and
# it carries the session cookie of whoever recorded it. Fixtures are written by hand from one,
# scrubbed, and committed under tests/; the capture itself never is.
*.har

# --- Secrets and local env ---
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- IDE / editor ---
# `.vscode/*` rather than `.vscode/`: a bare directory ignore stops git descending into it at all,
# so the negation below would be inert. The workspace file is the shared editor setup; everything
# else in there (settings.json and the like) is a personal override.
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/odouche.code-workspace
*.swp
*~

# --- OS noise ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- AI agents & tools ---
# The whole directory, not just the local settings: the hooks and skills kept there drive a
# private task tracker, and this repository is public. CLAUDE.md is the shared part.
/.claude/
.mcp.json
